{
  "id": "grammar.a1.01.a",
  "title": "Hungarian Vowels",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Short and long pairs",
      "content": "Hungarian vowels come in seven short/long pairs — *á*, *é*, *í*, *ó*, *ő*, *ú* and *ű* are not decoration but distinct sounds. For five of the pairs (*i*/*í*, *o*/*ó*, *ö*/*ő*, *u*/*ú*, *ü*/*ű*) the long version is just held longer, same sound. Two of them, *a*/*á* and *e*/*é*, also change quality: *a* is like British \"hot\", *á* is like \"father\"; *e* is like \"bet\", *é* is a purer, longer \"ay\" with no glide. And *ö*/*ő* and *ü*/*ű* don't exist in English at all: round your lips as if to say \"oh\" or \"oo\", but say \"eh\" or \"ee\" instead."
    },
